WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
A sourdough starter is not a recipe, it's an ecosystem, and that changes how you should treat it.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.400
Wild yeast and lactic acid bacteria are already living on the flour before you add any water.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.900
Feeding the starter isn't adding life to it, it's just giving the life that's already there more to eat.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:21.200
The ratio people obsess over, equal parts flour and water by weight, is a starting point, not a law.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.600
A stiffer starter, more flour than water, ferments slower and tastes less sour over time.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:32.000
A looser starter ferments faster and produces more of the acid that gives sourdough its name.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.400
Temperature matters more than almost anything else you can control in your kitchen.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.800
A starter at 20 degrees can take twice as long to peak as one at 28 degrees.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.100
That's why the same schedule that works for someone in summer fails for someone in winter.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:53.500
The float test, dropping a spoonful in water, tells you if it's gassy, not if it's ready.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:58.900
What actually predicts a good rise is watching the starter double and start to dome, then fall.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:04.300
Bake at the dome, right before the fall, and you'll stop wondering why some loaves come out dense.
